 @jaykay9975   - No, it isn't. And voting on your own posts doesn't change that. The framebuffer is (as the name suggests - it's really not that complicated), the part of video RAM that corresponds to the frame being displayed (or about to be displayed - for pageflipping) on screen, including RGBA data, Z-buffer, etc.. Its size is determined by the resolution, number of channels, and bit depth of each channel.

It would literally have taken you less than ten seconds to look that up - less than it took you to type that reply. 

The vast majority of the video RAM is used to store textures, models, shaders, etc.. None of that is part of the famebuffer, which is only "an actual term used for VRAM" by people who have no idea what they're talking about, and couldn't even invest those 10 seconds in their own education.

