 @chronometer9931  I understand Intel have stated they intend to continue and say so what. Statements are much different from being actually delivering in reality. I am entitled to query Intel's ability and willingness to continue given they made a loss last year, have struggled to compete against AMD in CPU and the world economy ought to slow down this year with the effect of central bank interest rate rises. This implies reduced demand for GPUs overall and they still need to spend a lot to get there - it would be entirely understandable (although to my mind, highly regretable) if they did.

But as it is, please note that my original comment referred to desktop GPUs, not GPUs in general, I was thinking rather than canning GPU development altogether, they might be tempted to make battlemage laptop only, perhaps returning to discrete desktop later once the economy improves and their cards sell without needing big discounts. Again, there's still a lot of investment required for that and it would be plausible to doubt whether they will actually see this through, although presumably they should aim to to profit from AI more.


Note I say all of this with the hope that they do continue to produce discrete desktop cards since as consumers we will benefit from (and desperately need) increased competition.
